keep the following rules in mind and we should be safe:

<> When changing the public api no worries eclipse will warn you accordingly so dont forget to bump the version#s in the bundle ( please
stop using import-packages immediately theres really no benefit its all marketing fud behind SOA )

<> The real worry is when upgrading one of the bundles that you depend upon. One of the two things can go bad:
   -> the dependent bundle in question has a public api that is also part of the public api of our bundle ( part of method params/
      return types and so forth ).  This means your own bundle version#s need to be bumped up

   -> the new upgraded bundle exposes a type in its public api that mismatches in version with the same type thats used as part
      of the public api in another dependent bundle.


   The first one is easy to catch, whenever you upgrade, just comment out that required-bundle statement and see if the build
   breaks in any of your public packages with errors in their public fields. if yes just cascade the version change upto your
   own bundle.

   The latter has no real solution we are simply counting on luck and manual inspection. These idiots at PDE dont even understand
   the magnitude of this problem.

<> Another thing, dont buy into all the marketing fud about SOA and services and dynamics, its really not that easy to do with
   Java, heck even Ruby cant get it right so stay away from it unless some new employer pays you for it.

<> AND most importantly, since we are using required-bundle and not import-package ( because bundles and bundles alone not
   packages truly represent a component, one could have a component with multiple packages ), if you wanted to have
   multiple vendors implement the same component keep the following rules in mind and it is still possible:

   -> The component interface declaration should only be part of one bundle. For example the IComponent and public interfaces
      should be in a single component ( Java doesnt make it possible to extend a package from a different JAR )

   -> The config packages can be in separate bundles. The new vendor needs to require-bundle the main one and implement its
      own config classes. It could extend the main vendor's config classes if it chose to.

   -> VERY VERY IMPORTANT!! Always keep the version#s of the different vendors in sync. So if you make a change in your main
      interface and bump the version to 9.0.0 then when the new vendor uses thsi change make sure its at 9.0.0 too. This will
      make a lot of detections/mistakes easy to catch with visual inspections.